1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,117
Lift a bucket of water.

2
00:00:02,117 --> 00:00:04,284
Push a cupboard across the room.

3
00:00:04,284 --> 00:00:05,853
Pull a cart of books.

4
00:00:06,253 --> 00:00:09,054
In daily life, we call
all three hard work.

5
00:00:09,054 --> 00:00:10,611
But physics is stricter.

6
00:00:11,061 --> 00:00:14,547
Diya lifts her eight kilogram
bag onto a shelf, one metre up.

7
00:00:14,547 --> 00:00:17,288
Then she carries it ten
metres down the corridor.

8
00:00:17,638 --> 00:00:19,442
Her arms ache both times.

9
00:00:19,442 --> 00:00:22,911
But physics says she did
work on the bag only once.

10
00:00:22,911 --> 00:00:23,833
Which time?

11
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:26,483
One small formula will tell us.

12
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:28,842
So what is work in physics?

13
00:00:29,192 --> 00:00:31,711
Work is done when a force moves something.

14
00:00:31,711 --> 00:00:34,372
A force must act, and the thing must move.

15
00:00:34,772 --> 00:00:36,395
Push a wall all day.

16
00:00:36,395 --> 00:00:37,797
It does not move.

17
00:00:37,797 --> 00:00:41,366
So you do no work on it,
however tired you feel.

18
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:45,040
And only the part of the
force along the motion counts.

19
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,527
That is the shadow from the last episode.

20
00:00:47,927 --> 00:00:51,723
So work is force, times
displacement, times cos theta.

21
00:00:52,373 --> 00:00:53,828
Let's measure work live.

22
00:00:53,828 --> 00:00:56,213
Diya drags a box of books with a rope.

23
00:00:56,213 --> 00:00:57,987
She pulls with fifty newtons.

24
00:00:58,337 --> 00:01:01,519
First, the rope is flat, so theta is zero.

25
00:01:01,519 --> 00:01:03,104
Watch the work meter.

26
00:01:03,504 --> 00:01:05,276
One metre, fifty joules.

27
00:01:05,276 --> 00:01:07,048
Two metres, one hundred.

28
00:01:07,048 --> 00:01:09,275
Four metres, two hundred joules.

29
00:01:09,675 --> 00:01:11,412
Now she pulls at sixty degrees.

30
00:01:11,412 --> 00:01:13,888
The same fifty newtons,
the same four metres.

31
00:01:14,288 --> 00:01:17,416
This time the meter stops
at one hundred joules.

32
00:01:17,416 --> 00:01:19,995
Only half her pull is along the floor.

33
00:01:20,495 --> 00:01:21,822
Slow-mo replay.

34
00:01:22,172 --> 00:01:23,668
F is along the rope.

35
00:01:23,668 --> 00:01:27,144
Its forward part is F cos
theta, twenty five newtons.

36
00:01:27,144 --> 00:01:28,737
The box moves along d.

37
00:01:29,237 --> 00:01:31,937
On the graph, work grows
in a straight line.

38
00:01:31,937 --> 00:01:34,139
The flat rope gives the steeper line.

39
00:01:34,789 --> 00:01:37,569
Now the formula, one small step at a time.

40
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:38,819
Step one.

41
00:01:38,813 --> 00:01:43,295
Work is the forward part of the
force, times the displacement.

42
00:01:43,295 --> 00:01:45,771
W equals F cos theta, times d.

43
00:01:46,521 --> 00:01:47,425
Step two.

44
00:01:47,425 --> 00:01:49,753
That is exactly a dot product.

45
00:01:49,753 --> 00:01:51,298
W equals F dot d.

46
00:01:52,048 --> 00:01:52,948
Step three.

47
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:57,314
Force and displacement are vectors,
but their dot product is a number.

48
00:01:57,314 --> 00:01:58,800
So work is a scalar.

49
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:00,373
A size, no direction.

50
00:02:01,123 --> 00:02:02,023
Step four.

51
00:02:01,939 --> 00:02:06,468
One newton, moving something one metre
along it, does one joule of work.

52
00:02:06,468 --> 00:02:09,665
Its dimensions are ML
squared T to the minus two.

53
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:11,376
Remember this.

54
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:13,685
Work needs a force and a displacement.

55
00:02:13,685 --> 00:02:16,740
Only the part of the force
along the motion counts.

56
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,147
Now, back to Diya's bag.

57
00:02:19,147 --> 00:02:20,171
Side by side.

58
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:21,421
Lifting.

59
00:02:21,245 --> 00:02:25,088
Her force is up, the bag
moves up, so theta is zero.

60
00:02:25,088 --> 00:02:26,362
Work is m g h.

61
00:02:26,362 --> 00:02:31,112
Eight, times nine point eight, times
one, about seventy eight joules.

62
00:02:31,512 --> 00:02:32,412
Carrying.

63
00:02:32,186 --> 00:02:35,378
Her hand still pushes up,
but the bag moves sideways.

64
00:02:35,378 --> 00:02:38,296
Theta is ninety degrees,
and cos ninety is zero.

65
00:02:38,296 --> 00:02:40,317
So her work on the bag is zero.

66
00:02:40,917 --> 00:02:41,817
Now you try.

67
00:02:41,768 --> 00:02:44,230
Diya holds the bag
still, for five minutes.

68
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:45,164
Her arms ache.

69
00:02:45,164 --> 00:02:47,345
How much work does she do on the bag?

70
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:48,361
Pause and think.

71
00:02:51,561 --> 00:02:52,461
Zero!

72
00:02:52,157 --> 00:02:55,016
The bag does not move, so d is zero.

73
00:02:55,016 --> 00:03:00,172
Her muscles use energy inside her
body, but she does no work on the bag.

74
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:03,243
So how do you know if a force does work?

75
00:03:03,243 --> 00:03:04,373
Check three links.

76
00:03:04,723 --> 00:03:05,984
Is there a force?

77
00:03:05,984 --> 00:03:07,386
Did the object move?

78
00:03:07,386 --> 00:03:09,862
Is some of the force along the motion?

79
00:03:10,262 --> 00:03:11,459
Pushing a wall.

80
00:03:11,459 --> 00:03:12,493
Force, yes.

81
00:03:12,493 --> 00:03:13,477
Moved, no.

82
00:03:13,477 --> 00:03:14,377
No work.

83
00:03:14,658 --> 00:03:16,345
Carrying the bag sideways.

84
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:17,305
Force, yes.

85
00:03:17,305 --> 00:03:18,266
Moved, yes.

86
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:19,812
Along the motion, no.

87
00:03:19,812 --> 00:03:20,712
No work.

88
00:03:20,937 --> 00:03:22,109
Dragging the box.

89
00:03:22,109 --> 00:03:23,564
Yes, yes, and yes.

90
00:03:23,564 --> 00:03:24,553
Work is done.

91
00:03:25,203 --> 00:03:26,893
Now, one worked example.

92
00:03:27,243 --> 00:03:31,661
A force of six i plus two j newtons
moves a box from the point one, two,

93
00:03:31,661 --> 00:03:34,038
to the point five, five, in metres.

94
00:03:34,038 --> 00:03:35,344
Find the work done.

95
00:03:35,844 --> 00:03:37,601
First, the displacement.

96
00:03:37,601 --> 00:03:39,053
Final minus initial.

97
00:03:39,053 --> 00:03:42,454
Five minus one is four,
five minus two is three.

98
00:03:42,454 --> 00:03:44,617
So d is four i plus three j.

99
00:03:45,367 --> 00:03:47,319
Now, W equals F dot d.

100
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:49,594
Six times four is twenty four.

101
00:03:49,594 --> 00:03:51,427
Two times three is six.

102
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:53,077
Add them.

103
00:03:52,868 --> 00:03:54,687
The work done is thirty joules.

104
00:03:55,337 --> 00:03:56,798
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:03:57,148 --> 00:03:59,771
A rope makes thirty
degrees with the vertical.

106
00:03:59,771 --> 00:04:02,359
Many students write W
equals F d cos thirty.

107
00:04:02,709 --> 00:04:05,456
But theta is measured from the motion.

108
00:04:05,456 --> 00:04:09,580
The box moves along the floor,
so theta is sixty degrees.

109
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,461
W equals F d cos sixty.

110
00:04:12,111 --> 00:04:13,564
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:13,564 --> 00:04:18,558
Work as F dot d with vectors, a pull at
an angle, and the unit and dimensions

112
00:04:18,558 --> 00:04:19,458
of work.

113
00:04:19,909 --> 00:04:21,059
Exam level now.

114
00:04:21,059 --> 00:04:25,841
A ten kilogram box is pulled five metres
at a steady speed on a rough floor,

115
00:04:25,841 --> 00:04:29,104
by a rope at thirty seven
degrees above the floor.

116
00:04:29,104 --> 00:04:31,626
Mu is point seven five and g is ten.

117
00:04:31,626 --> 00:04:35,785
Cos thirty seven is point eight,
sine thirty seven is point six.

118
00:04:35,785 --> 00:04:37,947
Find the work done by the rope.

119
00:04:37,947 --> 00:04:39,145
Pause and solve.

120
00:04:44,545 --> 00:04:45,931
The answer is B.

121
00:04:45,931 --> 00:04:48,079
Two hundred and forty joules.

122
00:04:48,579 --> 00:04:51,159
Steady speed, so the forces balance.

123
00:04:51,159 --> 00:04:53,163
F cos theta equals friction.

124
00:04:53,663 --> 00:04:57,696
Friction is mu N, and N
is m g minus F sin theta.

125
00:04:58,196 --> 00:05:02,761
So point eight F equals point seven
five, times one hundred minus point

126
00:05:02,761 --> 00:05:03,661
six F.

127
00:05:03,443 --> 00:05:04,910
F is sixty newtons.

128
00:05:05,410 --> 00:05:07,739
The forward part is forty eight newtons.

129
00:05:07,739 --> 00:05:11,009
Times five metres, that is
two hundred and forty joules.

130
00:05:11,609 --> 00:05:13,149
Here is your recap.

131
00:05:13,149 --> 00:05:14,732
Screenshot this card.

132
00:05:17,932 --> 00:05:20,118
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

133
00:05:20,518 --> 00:05:21,418
One.

134
00:05:20,923 --> 00:05:23,637
Why does pushing a wall
all day do no work on it?

135
00:05:24,537 --> 00:05:25,437
Two.

136
00:05:25,022 --> 00:05:29,027
Why is work a scalar, though force
and displacement are vectors?

137
00:05:29,927 --> 00:05:30,827
Three.

138
00:05:30,535 --> 00:05:33,206
What is one joule, in your own words?

139
00:05:34,306 --> 00:05:38,825
Next time: brakes, a ceiling fan,
and books carried down a corridor.

140
00:05:38,825 --> 00:05:42,155
Positive work, negative
work, and no work at all.

141
00:05:42,155 --> 00:05:45,339
We find out in positive,
negative and zero work.
